 @ranchdressin-d3b  Doesn't work like that anymore.

See, the problem with current forest fires is that in ye olden days when burns were more frequent trees - protected by bark and layers and old growth, could survive. Now fires burn so hot they kill the big trees - the ones we want to keep around and the ones that are the linchpin of a healthy ecosystem. What grows up in their place is shallow, fast-growing scrub brush and small trees that create an ecosystem that isn't nearly as healthy and is more prone to fires and erosion.

To put this is a capitalist analogy: too many businesses failing at once shatters the economy and leaves no wealth left to grow new businesses; all that can grow up are subsistence businesses catering to desperately poor people. It's a big reason a lot of 3rd world economies just can't get out of the cycle of debt and poverty - there is literally no wealth in the country and no way to build anyway.

